11.text:
In air the (exposure) ionisation current is

\begin{equation*}
\dot Q =I =\frac{\dot E_{a}}{W/e}\qquad 
\dot E_{a}=I\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

is the energy actually absorbed per unit time in the air which is
illuminated by the focused beam.  For $W_{air}\simeq 33.97\;{\rm eV}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times 33.97\;{\rm V}=6.8\times
10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

is the power absorbed in the air volume which is in the beam.
The air which is illuminated is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}\,h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times
10^{-2}\,{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{a}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272
{\rm cm^{3}}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
      =\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}\approx
      \frac{6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm J/s}}{3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}}
      \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{tissue}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$
the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue at the focus is the same to first
order

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\approx\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy/s}
\end{equation*}

in the focal spot.  On a scanned micro-beam the time a given tissue
element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ beam is the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;speed}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{(\Delta x/t_{\rm exp})}
\end{equation*}

which is quoted for the present scan as

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

per energy step.  The dose deposited in that element in one energy
step is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx\dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

about $3$–$4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per imaging position, i.e. $\approx
3.9\times10^{-3}\,$mGy per energy step, essentially the air kerma rate
times the 20 ms dwell.  In the usual presentation

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq0.19\;{\rm mGy/s}\times0.020\;{\rm s}\simeq3.9\times10^{-3}
\;{\rm mGy}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per focal spot per energy, the same to within the $(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ 
tissue$\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)$air approximation.  

If the dose is integrated over the whole $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$
field which is covered by scanning the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot, 
$N\simeq 50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$ steps are required.  The total
time of the scan is $N\,t_{d}\approx3.3\;{\rm s}$ and the energy
integrated over the whole scanned line is $N D_{1}\approx
0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}$ to the scanned surface, about $0.64\;{\rm mGy}$
for the nominal $167$ steps, i.e. $\sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per step, the
conventional quoted “cumulative’’ surface dose for the Laue
micro-beam at 33 keV.  The $0.02\,{\rm s}$ dwell is the $w_{f}/
\Delta x\,t_{\rm exp}$ overlap quoted in the statement; the $50\,{\rm mm}
\rightarrow0.30\,{\rm mm}$ demagnification is the number of scan steps
required to cover the monochromator illuminated field. ∎

5.text:
In the air the charge which is collected in the chamber is

\begin{equation*}
\dot Q=I=\frac {\dot E_{\rm dep}}{W/e}
\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
\dot E_{\rm dep}=I\,\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the volume which is actually illuminated
by the  $0.3\times 6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$  focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{\rm air}
    =\frac{\dot E_{\rm dep}}{m_{\rm air}}
    =\frac{I}{A_{f}L\rho_{\rm air}}\,\frac{W}{e}  ,
\end{equation*}

$A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\,{\rm
cm^{2}}$ is the focus area and $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ the length of the
chamber along the beam.  

\begin{equation*}
m_{\rm air}= \rho_{\rm air}A_{f}L
       =1.293\times10^{-3}\,{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.018\,{\rm cm^{2}}
         \times15.1\,{\rm cm}
       \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm \;g}
\end{equation*}

With $W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{\rm dep}=I\,W/e =2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\,{\rm V}
              \simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{\rm air}
   =\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}}{3.52\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}}
   \simeq1.9\text{--}2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
   \simeq0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
in air at the focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{\rm tissue}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{\rm air}$ this is the absorbed dose rate in soft
tissue at the focus,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

The tissue point which is carried through the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$
horizontal focus stays in the beam for

\begin{equation*}
\Delta t=\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}
       =\frac{w_{f}}{\rm scan\;step/exp\;time}
\end{equation*}

and the data are quoted as

\begin{equation*}
\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

i.e. a dwell time of about $2\times10^{-2}\,$s per beam crossing.  The
absorbed dose delivered to a given surface element on a single
energy/exposure is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq\dot D_{t}\,\Delta t
      \simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
      \simeq3.8\text{--}4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

i.e. about $3$--$4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx4\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$ per
beam crossing per energy.  This is the usual “point’’ or
instantaneous surface dose quoted for the $33\;{\rm keV}$ focus.

If the dose is integrated over the whole field which is swept out by
the $50\,{\rm mm}$ monochromator beam, $N_{b}=w_{m}/w_{f}\approx
50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$ beam widths are swept.  The integrated
surface exposure of the scanned line is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm int}\approx N_{b}D_{1}
       \approx166.7\times3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}
       \approx6\text{--}7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\;{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, i.e. $\sim0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ for the $50\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal
field, essentially the $ \approx0.6$ mGy total air/tissue kerma
delivered to the surface during the complete horizontal scan.  The
$3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $0.02\,{\rm s}$ beam crossing is the often-quoted
$\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per exposure surface dose at the $33\;{\rm keV}$
synchrotron focus.  With $n_{E}$ energies this is $n_{E}D_{1}$ per
point, $\,n_{E}\times\sim0.6\,{\rm mGy}$ integrated over the 50 mm
field.

10.text:
The current which is collected in the air of the chamber is the ionisation
produced by the energy which is actually deposited in the air of the
chamber

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{\rm dep}= \frac{I}{e}\,W_{air}\qquad 
W_{air}\simeq 33.9\;{\rm eV/ion~pair}
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{\rm dep}=\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm C\,s^{-1}}}{1.602\times10^{-19}{\rm C}}
  \times33.97\;{\rm eV}\times1.602\times10^{-19}{\rm J/eV}
      \approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm J\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

For the part of the chamber which is illuminated at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

the mass of air which is seen by the beam is, with the conventional
definition $L$ the depth of air along the beam,

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}\,A_{f}L
     =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.018\,{\rm cm^{2}}
      \times15.1\,{\rm cm}
     \approx 3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg},
\end{equation*}

of air which is equivalent to tissue to the accuracy required here.
The peak air–kerma rate at the focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{\rm dep}}{m_{a}}
          =\frac{I\,W_{air}}{e\,m_{a}}
          \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air and, with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$,
essentially the same absorbed dose rate in tissue.  In terms of
fluence

\begin{equation*}
\dot D =\Phi\,E_{0}\,\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{a},
\qquad 
\Phi =\frac{\dot E_{dep}}{E_{0}m_{a}(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}
      \approx1\times10^{12}\;{\rm m^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

which is the usual $\dot D=\Phi E_{0}(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ form, the
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV being used
only to check the consistency.

A surface element of the specimen stays in the 0.30 mm horizontal fan
for a dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}\approx0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the value quoted as the beam width / scan speed.  The absorbed dose
deposited in that element during one pass through the fan, and the same
dose is delivered in one energy step of the Laue multi–energy system,
is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{a}\,t_{d}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
       \times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}\approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. about $3$–$4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\simeq3\!-\!4\times10^{-3}\;{\rm mGy}$ per
20 ms projection,  $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak air–kerma rate
at the focus.  This is the dose seen by a given point of the surface
on a single energy exposure.  The dose scales linearly with the number
of energy steps used in the multi-energy acquisition, $D_{N}\approx
N\,D_{1}$, and is normally quoted as a few $\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection,
$\sim 10^{-3}\,$mGy, in the surface layer of soft tissue under the
conditions stated.  Using $W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$ the often quoted
$0.0038\;{\rm mGy}$ $\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 0.02 s dwell is obtained,
i.e. $\approx0.2\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous dose rate at the
focus, essentially the conventional low-dose synchrotron imaging level.

6.text:
For an air filled chamber the charge which is collected per second is

${\rm d}Q/{\rm d}t=I$  so that the ion pairs created per second in the
volume which is irradiated is $I/e$ and the power which is absorbed in
the air in that volume is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{I\,W}{e}\simeq I\times 33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}
$W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm eV}$ per ion pair being the $W$ value of air, i.e.
$W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$.  With $I=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\;
{\rm W}
\end{equation*}
is the power absorbed in the air which is actually in the beam.

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30{\rm mm}\times6.0{\rm mm}=0.018\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}
so that with a chamber depth $L=15.1{\rm cm}$ the mass of air which is
irradiated at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L
     =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.018{\rm cm^{2}}
       \times15.1{\rm cm}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}
     =3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the air kerma rate in the focal plane is

\begin{equation*}
\dot k_{air}= \frac{\dot K_{air}}{m_{a}}
      =\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}{\rm J\,s^{-1}}}{3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm
        kg}}\approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\approx0.19{\rm  mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the absorbed dose rate
in soft tissue in the focal plane is essentially the same,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot k_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}
In fact

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}= \Phi_{0}E\,\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{t}
\end{equation*}
with

\begin{equation*}
\Phi_{0}= \frac{\dot K_{air}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}m_{a}}
       \approx1.1\times10^{12}\ {\rm m^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at 33 keV, $E=5.29\times10^{-15}{\rm J}$, $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$.

A material point which is stepped through the focus stays in the
$0.30{\rm mm}$ wide focal spot for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{(\Delta x/\Delta t)}\approx0.02\;
{\rm s}
\end{equation*}
which is the dwell time quoted in the statement.  The dose delivered
to a single surface element during one passage through the monochromatic
focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}\approx3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}
i.e. of the order of a few microgray per energy passage, $\approx
3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx4\times10^{-3}{\rm mGy}$, to the surface on the
tissue side.  This is the conventional “one-energy’’ surface dose.
With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the air kerma is
essentially the tissue absorbed dose.

If the whole Laue bandwidth which is mapped by the bent crystal is
added up, the number of independent energy bins which are covered in
the horizontal direction is $N_{E}\approx w_{m}/w_{f}=50.0/0.30\approx
1.67\times10^{2}$.  The total dose which is accumulated while the
object is stepped through the complete dispersed beam is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{tot}\approx N_{E}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
{\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.5\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}
$\approx0.64\ {\rm mGy}$ on the surface for the full $50\,{\rm mm}$
Laue bandwidth.  The $3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic passage
is the usual quoted per-image surface dose; the $\sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$
figure is the integrated dose obtained when the $\approx 167$
independent energy steps of the Laue monochromator are summed.  The
$\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per passage is essentially independent of the
$50{\rm mm}\times6{\rm mm}$ incident size which is the usual red
herring – it is the $0.30{\rm mm}\times6{\rm mm}$ focal size which sets
the mass of air that is actually irradiated. ∎

2.text:
In an air ionisation chamber the charge collected per second is
$e\dot N =\dot Q$ and  $\dot N$ ion pairs are produced with the mean
energy $W$ per ion pair

\begin{equation*}
\dot Q = e\,\dot N\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;  \dot E_{a}= \dot N\,W
       =\frac{W}{e}\,\dot Q .
\end{equation*}

With the conventional $W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}= \dot Q\,W/e =2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
          \simeq 6.8\times 10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

is the energy removed per second from the photon beam in the air which
intercepts the beam.  In the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with a chamber depth $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ the mass of air which is
in the beam at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L
     =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times
       1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}\times15.1{\rm cm}
     \approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg} ,
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the beam, which for $E=33\;{\rm keV}$ is
to a very good approximation the absorbed dose rate in air, is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{a}(0)=\frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
          =\frac{\dot Q\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
          \approx\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$  at the entrance of the chamber.
With

\begin{equation*}
k_{en}=(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho =0.328\,{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}
        \times1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}
        =4.24\times10^{-4}\ {\rm cm^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$k_{en}L\simeq6.4\times10^{-3}\ll1$, so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{a}(x)=\dot D_{a}(0)e^{-k_{en}x}\approx\dot D_{a}(0) 
\end{equation*}

and the average dose over the $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ of air,

\begin{equation*}
\langle \dot D_{a}\rangle
 =\dot D_{a}(0)\frac{1-e^{-k_{en}L}}{k_{en}L}
 \approx \dot D_{a}(0)\left[1+{\cal O}(10^{-3})\right],
\end{equation*}

is essentially the entrance kerma quoted above.  For tissue
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ so that the absorbed
dose rate in tissue at the focal plane is the same as the air kerma
rate at the entrance of the chamber,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\approx\dot D_{a}(0)\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the instantaneous beam.

In a scanning micro-beam the time a given surface element spends in
the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focal spot is the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v}
     =\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. the beam width divided by the scan speed.  In the notation of the
statement $t_{d}=w_{f}/(\Delta x/t_{e})=0.02\;{\rm s}$.  The dose
delivered to a surface element during one passage of the spot is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

of absorbed dose in tissue at the surface, essentially air kerma, per
energy step per passage of the spot.  This is $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}\approx
0.4\,{\rm mrad}$ of surface dose at a single energy.  The $1-e^{-kL}$
correction for attenuation in the $15.1\,{\rm cm}$ of air is
$1-e^{-kL}\simeq kL=6.4\times10^{-3}$, i.e. $<1\%$, so the often
quoted $ \dot D_{a}\approx \dot QW/e\,/\,m_{a}$ is sufficient.

In practice the multiple-energy scan is repeated at $N_{E}$ energies.
The dose per scanned point then scales as $N_{E}D_{1}$, e.g.
$N_{E}=3$ gives $\sim 1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\approx12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
to the point that is illuminated.  Averaged over the $50\,{\rm mm}$ 
horizontal field which is imaged with the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot
the mean dose over the whole $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ field is
$D_{1}/M_{h}$ with $M_{h}=50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$, i.e. of
order $2\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}$ per energy step for the whole field,
the conventional $\,\sim 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ being the local surface dose
in the instantaneous focal spot.  This is the order of magnitude
normally quoted for a synchrotron Laue micro-beam multi-energy scan.

12.text:
The air kerma rate that can be obtained from the ionisation current is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{{\rm d}E/{\rm d}t}{m_{a}}
      =\frac{I}{e}\,\frac{W}{m_{a}}   ,
\end{equation*}
$W\simeq 33.97\;{\rm eV}$ being the mean energy per ion pair in air.
In the beam the mass of air which is ionised is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}\;A_{f}\;L,
\qquad 
A_{f}=0.30\times 6.0~{\rm mm^{2}}=1.8\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm \frac{g}{cm^{3}}}\times
0.018\,{\rm cm^{2}}\times15.1\,{\rm cm}\approx 3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}.
\end{equation*}

$I/e$ is the number of ion pairs per second,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{I}{e}=\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}\ {\rm A}}{1.602\times10^{-19}\ {\rm C}}
      \approx1.25\times10^{7}\ {\rm s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
\frac{{\rm d}E}{{\rm d}t}= \frac{I}{e}W\approx
1.25\times10^{7}\times5.44\times10^{-18}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}.
\end{equation*}

Hence the instantaneous air-kerma rate at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}}{3.52\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}}
      \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

$\,\dot K_{a}= \Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ with
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV.
With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the same air-kerma is
obtained in soft tissue.

For a continuously scanned beam the dwell time of a given surface
element is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{v}
      =\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}
\end{equation*}
i.e. the beam width divided by the scan speed.  In the notation of the
statement

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{\dot x}=0.02\;{\rm s},
\end{equation*}

so that the dose actually delivered to a surface element which is
seen once by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
K_{s}= \dot K_{a}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx 3$–$4\,\mu {\rm Gy}$ to the skin surface per energy
view,  $\approx 0.2\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous air kerma rate at
the focus. The $\approx0.0064$ fractional energy absorption in the
15.1 cm of air is $\ll1$, so the same number is the incident air
kerma rate at the surface.  With $n_{E}$ distinct monochromatic views
the total surface dose is $n_{E}K_{s}$; e.g. for the usual two–three
energy DE–/multiple–energy set $K_{tot}\sim 8$–$12\;\mu {\rm Gy}$,
essentially the few $\mu$Gy per image level which is normal for
synchrotron multiple–energy radiography.  This is the dose per
illuminated surface element; the quoted $\approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
is the instantaneous air–tissue kerma rate at the focus.

*Remark*  The $50\,{\rm mm}\!\times\!6\,{\rm mm}$ to $0.30\,{\rm mm}
\!\times\!6\,{\rm mm}$ demagnification is $50/0.30\approx 167$; the
product $\Phi A$ and hence the total photon power measured by the
chamber is conserved, so the $50\,{\rm mm}$ incident size enters only the
geometrical overlap factor already contained in $t_{d}=w_{f}/v$.
Using $W=34\,{\rm eV}$ the often quoted round number is $K_{s}\approx
3.9\;\mu$Gy per 20 ms dwell, $\approx 0.004\,{\rm mGy}$ per single
energy image. ∎

1.text:
For an air filled ion chamber the charge which is collected in the time $dt$
is

\begin{equation*}
dQ=e\,dN_{i}=e\,\frac{dE_{abs}}{W}
\end{equation*}

so that with the usual $W_{air}\simeq 33.7\,{\rm eV}= 33.7\,{\rm V}$
for air

\begin{equation*}
P_{abs}= {\dot E}_{abs}=I\,W\qquad 
{\rm  in~W}
\end{equation*}

is the power absorbed in the air which is actually traversed by the
beam.  For a beam which is small compared with the chamber

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}\,A_{f}\,L_{c}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is in the beam at any instant, $A_{f}$ the
geometrical cross section of the beam at the focus and $L_{c}$ the
length of air which is traversed by the beam.  With

* $A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\,{\rm
cm^{2}}$,
* $L_{c}=15.1\,{\rm cm}$,
* $\rho_{air}=1.293\times10^{-3}\,{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}$,

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1
      =3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is being ionised.  The power absorbed in that
mass is

\begin{equation*}
P_{abs}=I\,W=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.7{\rm V}
       \approx6.7\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{air}= \frac{P_{abs}}{m_{air}}
          =\frac{I\,W}{ \rho_{air}A_{f}L_{c}}
          \simeq\frac{6.7\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{tissue}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$
the same kerma rate is obtained in soft tissue to a very good
approximation, the usual $(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ cancelling in the ratio of
kerma to ionisation current,
\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq \dot D_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
        \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,min^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at the focal spot. In terms of a photon fluence $\Phi$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi =\frac{I\,W}{eA_{f}E(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho L_{c}}
     \simeq1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at 33 keV, i.e. $\dot K\simeq\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ as above.

A material point which is scanned under the 0.30 mm focal spot is
illuminated for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v}
     =\frac{w_{f}}{\dot s}
\end{equation*}

where $v$ is the scan speed.  The experiment is quoted as

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/{\rm exposure\;time}}=0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

so that the dose actually received by a surface element during one
energy pass is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. about $3$–$4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic exposure at the
focal spot.  With $W_{air}=33.97{\rm eV}$ the conventional number is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, essentially independent of the quoted $(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ as
long as $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{tissue}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$.  This
is the peak surface dose under the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focal
spot.

For a conventional dual–energy Laue acquisition the dose is
$2D_{1}\simeq7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ and for a three–energy material
decomposition acquisition $3D_{1}\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm
Gy}\sim10\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per scanned point, i.e. a few $10^{-5}$ Gy
total, which is the order of magnitude normally quoted for
synchrotron multi-energy micro–tomography.  The $50\,{\rm mm}$ incident
beam is the field which is covered by the scan, the $0.30/50\simeq
6\times10^{-3}$ geometric dilution giving a mean dose over the $50\times
6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ field of $D_{1}w_{f}/W_{0}\simeq2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\rm
Gy\approx20\!-\!30\,nGy$ per energy, essentially the conventional
“average over the imaged area” figure.  The $3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
energy quoted above is the local surface dose under the focal spot
which is the quantity normally used in radiation protection for the
scan. ∎

9.text:
In an air filled chamber the charge which is collected per unit time is

\begin{equation*}
I=\dot Q =\dot N_{i}\,e
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}= \dot N_{i}W = I\,W/e
\end{equation*}

is the rate at which energy is put into the air which is seen by the
beam.  With the usual $W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm eV}$ per ion pair

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}= I\frac{W}{e}= I\times33.97\;{\rm V}
        =2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97=6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

is the power which is recorded in the chamber.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with the $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ air column which is seen

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L =0.2718\;{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad 
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.2718
      =3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the chamber is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{air}}{m_{air}}
           =\frac{IW/e}{m_{air}}
           \approx\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
           \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $\mu_{en}/\rho_{t}\simeq\mu_{en}/\rho_{air}$ the
absorbed dose rate in tissue at the focus is essentially the same
air kerma rate.

For a horizontal scan the dwell time of a given point on the surface
under the stationary beam is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{\dot s}= \frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/t_{exp}}
\end{equation*}

and the quoted

\begin{equation*}
\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/t_{exp}}=0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

is the dwell time.  Hence the dose delivered to one illuminated
spot in one energy acquisition is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq\dot K_{air}\,t_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\sim 4\;\mu$Gy per beam dwell at the surface, essentially the
air kerma rate quoted above.  This is the conventional $\simeq$ 
$3\!-\!4\;\mu$Gy per mono-energetic exposure which is normally quoted
for a 33 keV Laue beam of the quoted intensity.

If the dose is integrated over the field which is covered by a
horizontal scan the number of distinct beam positions needed to cover
the 50 mm illumination at the monochromator is

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}\approx\frac{50.0\;{\rm mm}}{0.30\;{\rm mm}}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

so that the integrated energy put into the $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$
field in one energy acquisition is $N_{h}D_{1}$.  In integrated terms

\begin{equation*}
D_{int}\approx N_{h}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
       \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}$ absorbed in the $3\,{\rm cm^{2}}$
surface which is scanned in one mono-energetic pass.  For the usual
two–three energy multi-energy acquisition the point dose is $nD_{1}$,
e.g. $\approx7.7\;\mu$Gy for a dual energy acquisition and
$\approx11\!-\!12\;\mu$Gy for a three energy acquisition, the
integrated 50 mm field being $\approx1.3\!-\!2.0\;{\rm mGy}$,
respectively.  The $\sim4\;\mu$Gy per dwell is the often quoted
surface kerma per mono-energetic image.

public_text.11:
In air the (exposure) ionisation current is

\begin{equation*}
\dot Q =I =\frac{\dot E_{a}}{W/e}\qquad 
\dot E_{a}=I\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

is the energy actually absorbed per unit time in the air which is
illuminated by the focused beam.  For $W_{air}\simeq 33.97\;{\rm eV}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times 33.97\;{\rm V}=6.8\times
10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

is the power absorbed in the air volume which is in the beam.
The air which is illuminated is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}\,h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times
10^{-2}\,{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{a}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272
{\rm cm^{3}}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
      =\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}\approx
      \frac{6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm J/s}}{3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}}
      \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{tissue}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$
the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue at the focus is the same to first
order

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\approx\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy/s}
\end{equation*}

in the focal spot.  On a scanned micro-beam the time a given tissue
element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ beam is the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;speed}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{(\Delta x/t_{\rm exp})}
\end{equation*}

which is quoted for the present scan as

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

per energy step.  The dose deposited in that element in one energy
step is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx\dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

about $3$–$4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per imaging position, i.e. $\approx
3.9\times10^{-3}\,$mGy per energy step, essentially the air kerma rate
times the 20 ms dwell.  In the usual presentation

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq0.19\;{\rm mGy/s}\times0.020\;{\rm s}\simeq3.9\times10^{-3}
\;{\rm mGy}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per focal spot per energy, the same to within the $(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ 
tissue$\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)$air approximation.  

If the dose is integrated over the whole $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$
field which is covered by scanning the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot, 
$N\simeq 50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$ steps are required.  The total
time of the scan is $N\,t_{d}\approx3.3\;{\rm s}$ and the energy
integrated over the whole scanned line is $N D_{1}\approx
0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}$ to the scanned surface, about $0.64\;{\rm mGy}$
for the nominal $167$ steps, i.e. $\sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per step, the
conventional quoted “cumulative’’ surface dose for the Laue
micro-beam at 33 keV.  The $0.02\,{\rm s}$ dwell is the $w_{f}/
\Delta x\,t_{\rm exp}$ overlap quoted in the statement; the $50\,{\rm mm}
\rightarrow0.30\,{\rm mm}$ demagnification is the number of scan steps
required to cover the monochromator illuminated field. ∎

public_text.5:
In the air the charge which is collected in the chamber is

\begin{equation*}
\dot Q=I=\frac {\dot E_{\rm dep}}{W/e}
\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
\dot E_{\rm dep}=I\,\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the volume which is actually illuminated
by the  $0.3\times 6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$  focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{\rm air}
    =\frac{\dot E_{\rm dep}}{m_{\rm air}}
    =\frac{I}{A_{f}L\rho_{\rm air}}\,\frac{W}{e}  ,
\end{equation*}

$A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\,{\rm
cm^{2}}$ is the focus area and $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ the length of the
chamber along the beam.  

\begin{equation*}
m_{\rm air}= \rho_{\rm air}A_{f}L
       =1.293\times10^{-3}\,{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.018\,{\rm cm^{2}}
         \times15.1\,{\rm cm}
       \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm \;g}
\end{equation*}

With $W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{\rm dep}=I\,W/e =2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\,{\rm V}
              \simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{\rm air}
   =\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}}{3.52\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}}
   \simeq1.9\text{--}2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
   \simeq0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
in air at the focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{\rm tissue}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{\rm air}$ this is the absorbed dose rate in soft
tissue at the focus,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

The tissue point which is carried through the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$
horizontal focus stays in the beam for

\begin{equation*}
\Delta t=\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}
       =\frac{w_{f}}{\rm scan\;step/exp\;time}
\end{equation*}

and the data are quoted as

\begin{equation*}
\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

i.e. a dwell time of about $2\times10^{-2}\,$s per beam crossing.  The
absorbed dose delivered to a given surface element on a single
energy/exposure is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq\dot D_{t}\,\Delta t
      \simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
      \simeq3.8\text{--}4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

i.e. about $3$--$4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx4\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$ per
beam crossing per energy.  This is the usual “point’’ or
instantaneous surface dose quoted for the $33\;{\rm keV}$ focus.

If the dose is integrated over the whole field which is swept out by
the $50\,{\rm mm}$ monochromator beam, $N_{b}=w_{m}/w_{f}\approx
50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$ beam widths are swept.  The integrated
surface exposure of the scanned line is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm int}\approx N_{b}D_{1}
       \approx166.7\times3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}
       \approx6\text{--}7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\;{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, i.e. $\sim0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ for the $50\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal
field, essentially the $ \approx0.6$ mGy total air/tissue kerma
delivered to the surface during the complete horizontal scan.  The
$3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $0.02\,{\rm s}$ beam crossing is the often-quoted
$\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per exposure surface dose at the $33\;{\rm keV}$
synchrotron focus.  With $n_{E}$ energies this is $n_{E}D_{1}$ per
point, $\,n_{E}\times\sim0.6\,{\rm mGy}$ integrated over the 50 mm
field.

public_text.10:
The current which is collected in the air of the chamber is the ionisation
produced by the energy which is actually deposited in the air of the
chamber

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{\rm dep}= \frac{I}{e}\,W_{air}\qquad 
W_{air}\simeq 33.9\;{\rm eV/ion~pair}
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{\rm dep}=\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm C\,s^{-1}}}{1.602\times10^{-19}{\rm C}}
  \times33.97\;{\rm eV}\times1.602\times10^{-19}{\rm J/eV}
      \approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm J\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

For the part of the chamber which is illuminated at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

the mass of air which is seen by the beam is, with the conventional
definition $L$ the depth of air along the beam,

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}\,A_{f}L
     =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.018\,{\rm cm^{2}}
      \times15.1\,{\rm cm}
     \approx 3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg},
\end{equation*}

of air which is equivalent to tissue to the accuracy required here.
The peak air–kerma rate at the focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{\rm dep}}{m_{a}}
          =\frac{I\,W_{air}}{e\,m_{a}}
          \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air and, with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$,
essentially the same absorbed dose rate in tissue.  In terms of
fluence

\begin{equation*}
\dot D =\Phi\,E_{0}\,\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{a},
\qquad 
\Phi =\frac{\dot E_{dep}}{E_{0}m_{a}(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}
      \approx1\times10^{12}\;{\rm m^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

which is the usual $\dot D=\Phi E_{0}(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ form, the
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV being used
only to check the consistency.

A surface element of the specimen stays in the 0.30 mm horizontal fan
for a dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}\approx0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the value quoted as the beam width / scan speed.  The absorbed dose
deposited in that element during one pass through the fan, and the same
dose is delivered in one energy step of the Laue multi–energy system,
is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{a}\,t_{d}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
       \times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}\approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. about $3$–$4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\simeq3\!-\!4\times10^{-3}\;{\rm mGy}$ per
20 ms projection,  $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak air–kerma rate
at the focus.  This is the dose seen by a given point of the surface
on a single energy exposure.  The dose scales linearly with the number
of energy steps used in the multi-energy acquisition, $D_{N}\approx
N\,D_{1}$, and is normally quoted as a few $\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection,
$\sim 10^{-3}\,$mGy, in the surface layer of soft tissue under the
conditions stated.  Using $W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$ the often quoted
$0.0038\;{\rm mGy}$ $\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 0.02 s dwell is obtained,
i.e. $\approx0.2\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous dose rate at the
focus, essentially the conventional low-dose synchrotron imaging level.

public_text.6:
For an air filled chamber the charge which is collected per second is

${\rm d}Q/{\rm d}t=I$  so that the ion pairs created per second in the
volume which is irradiated is $I/e$ and the power which is absorbed in
the air in that volume is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{I\,W}{e}\simeq I\times 33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}
$W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm eV}$ per ion pair being the $W$ value of air, i.e.
$W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$.  With $I=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\;
{\rm W}
\end{equation*}
is the power absorbed in the air which is actually in the beam.

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30{\rm mm}\times6.0{\rm mm}=0.018\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}
so that with a chamber depth $L=15.1{\rm cm}$ the mass of air which is
irradiated at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L
     =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.018{\rm cm^{2}}
       \times15.1{\rm cm}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}
     =3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the air kerma rate in the focal plane is

\begin{equation*}
\dot k_{air}= \frac{\dot K_{air}}{m_{a}}
      =\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}{\rm J\,s^{-1}}}{3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm
        kg}}\approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\approx0.19{\rm  mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the absorbed dose rate
in soft tissue in the focal plane is essentially the same,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot k_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}
In fact

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}= \Phi_{0}E\,\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{t}
\end{equation*}
with

\begin{equation*}
\Phi_{0}= \frac{\dot K_{air}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}m_{a}}
       \approx1.1\times10^{12}\ {\rm m^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at 33 keV, $E=5.29\times10^{-15}{\rm J}$, $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$.

A material point which is stepped through the focus stays in the
$0.30{\rm mm}$ wide focal spot for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{(\Delta x/\Delta t)}\approx0.02\;
{\rm s}
\end{equation*}
which is the dwell time quoted in the statement.  The dose delivered
to a single surface element during one passage through the monochromatic
focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}\approx3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}
i.e. of the order of a few microgray per energy passage, $\approx
3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx4\times10^{-3}{\rm mGy}$, to the surface on the
tissue side.  This is the conventional “one-energy’’ surface dose.
With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the air kerma is
essentially the tissue absorbed dose.

If the whole Laue bandwidth which is mapped by the bent crystal is
added up, the number of independent energy bins which are covered in
the horizontal direction is $N_{E}\approx w_{m}/w_{f}=50.0/0.30\approx
1.67\times10^{2}$.  The total dose which is accumulated while the
object is stepped through the complete dispersed beam is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{tot}\approx N_{E}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
{\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.5\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}
$\approx0.64\ {\rm mGy}$ on the surface for the full $50\,{\rm mm}$
Laue bandwidth.  The $3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic passage
is the usual quoted per-image surface dose; the $\sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$
figure is the integrated dose obtained when the $\approx 167$
independent energy steps of the Laue monochromator are summed.  The
$\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per passage is essentially independent of the
$50{\rm mm}\times6{\rm mm}$ incident size which is the usual red
herring – it is the $0.30{\rm mm}\times6{\rm mm}$ focal size which sets
the mass of air that is actually irradiated. ∎

public_text.2:
In an air ionisation chamber the charge collected per second is
$e\dot N =\dot Q$ and  $\dot N$ ion pairs are produced with the mean
energy $W$ per ion pair

\begin{equation*}
\dot Q = e\,\dot N\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;  \dot E_{a}= \dot N\,W
       =\frac{W}{e}\,\dot Q .
\end{equation*}

With the conventional $W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}= \dot Q\,W/e =2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
          \simeq 6.8\times 10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

is the energy removed per second from the photon beam in the air which
intercepts the beam.  In the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with a chamber depth $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ the mass of air which is
in the beam at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L
     =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times
       1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}\times15.1{\rm cm}
     \approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg} ,
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the beam, which for $E=33\;{\rm keV}$ is
to a very good approximation the absorbed dose rate in air, is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{a}(0)=\frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
          =\frac{\dot Q\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
          \approx\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$  at the entrance of the chamber.
With

\begin{equation*}
k_{en}=(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho =0.328\,{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}
        \times1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}
        =4.24\times10^{-4}\ {\rm cm^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$k_{en}L\simeq6.4\times10^{-3}\ll1$, so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{a}(x)=\dot D_{a}(0)e^{-k_{en}x}\approx\dot D_{a}(0) 
\end{equation*}

and the average dose over the $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ of air,

\begin{equation*}
\langle \dot D_{a}\rangle
 =\dot D_{a}(0)\frac{1-e^{-k_{en}L}}{k_{en}L}
 \approx \dot D_{a}(0)\left[1+{\cal O}(10^{-3})\right],
\end{equation*}

is essentially the entrance kerma quoted above.  For tissue
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ so that the absorbed
dose rate in tissue at the focal plane is the same as the air kerma
rate at the entrance of the chamber,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\approx\dot D_{a}(0)\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the instantaneous beam.

In a scanning micro-beam the time a given surface element spends in
the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focal spot is the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v}
     =\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. the beam width divided by the scan speed.  In the notation of the
statement $t_{d}=w_{f}/(\Delta x/t_{e})=0.02\;{\rm s}$.  The dose
delivered to a surface element during one passage of the spot is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

of absorbed dose in tissue at the surface, essentially air kerma, per
energy step per passage of the spot.  This is $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}\approx
0.4\,{\rm mrad}$ of surface dose at a single energy.  The $1-e^{-kL}$
correction for attenuation in the $15.1\,{\rm cm}$ of air is
$1-e^{-kL}\simeq kL=6.4\times10^{-3}$, i.e. $<1\%$, so the often
quoted $ \dot D_{a}\approx \dot QW/e\,/\,m_{a}$ is sufficient.

In practice the multiple-energy scan is repeated at $N_{E}$ energies.
The dose per scanned point then scales as $N_{E}D_{1}$, e.g.
$N_{E}=3$ gives $\sim 1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\approx12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
to the point that is illuminated.  Averaged over the $50\,{\rm mm}$ 
horizontal field which is imaged with the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot
the mean dose over the whole $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ field is
$D_{1}/M_{h}$ with $M_{h}=50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$, i.e. of
order $2\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}$ per energy step for the whole field,
the conventional $\,\sim 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ being the local surface dose
in the instantaneous focal spot.  This is the order of magnitude
normally quoted for a synchrotron Laue micro-beam multi-energy scan.

public_text.12:
The air kerma rate that can be obtained from the ionisation current is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{{\rm d}E/{\rm d}t}{m_{a}}
      =\frac{I}{e}\,\frac{W}{m_{a}}   ,
\end{equation*}
$W\simeq 33.97\;{\rm eV}$ being the mean energy per ion pair in air.
In the beam the mass of air which is ionised is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}\;A_{f}\;L,
\qquad 
A_{f}=0.30\times 6.0~{\rm mm^{2}}=1.8\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm \frac{g}{cm^{3}}}\times
0.018\,{\rm cm^{2}}\times15.1\,{\rm cm}\approx 3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}.
\end{equation*}

$I/e$ is the number of ion pairs per second,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{I}{e}=\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}\ {\rm A}}{1.602\times10^{-19}\ {\rm C}}
      \approx1.25\times10^{7}\ {\rm s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}

so that

\begin{equation*}
\frac{{\rm d}E}{{\rm d}t}= \frac{I}{e}W\approx
1.25\times10^{7}\times5.44\times10^{-18}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}.
\end{equation*}

Hence the instantaneous air-kerma rate at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}}{3.52\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}}
      \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

$\,\dot K_{a}= \Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ with
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV.
With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the same air-kerma is
obtained in soft tissue.

For a continuously scanned beam the dwell time of a given surface
element is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{v}
      =\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}
\end{equation*}
i.e. the beam width divided by the scan speed.  In the notation of the
statement

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{\dot x}=0.02\;{\rm s},
\end{equation*}

so that the dose actually delivered to a surface element which is
seen once by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
K_{s}= \dot K_{a}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx 3$–$4\,\mu {\rm Gy}$ to the skin surface per energy
view,  $\approx 0.2\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous air kerma rate at
the focus. The $\approx0.0064$ fractional energy absorption in the
15.1 cm of air is $\ll1$, so the same number is the incident air
kerma rate at the surface.  With $n_{E}$ distinct monochromatic views
the total surface dose is $n_{E}K_{s}$; e.g. for the usual two–three
energy DE–/multiple–energy set $K_{tot}\sim 8$–$12\;\mu {\rm Gy}$,
essentially the few $\mu$Gy per image level which is normal for
synchrotron multiple–energy radiography.  This is the dose per
illuminated surface element; the quoted $\approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
is the instantaneous air–tissue kerma rate at the focus.

*Remark*  The $50\,{\rm mm}\!\times\!6\,{\rm mm}$ to $0.30\,{\rm mm}
\!\times\!6\,{\rm mm}$ demagnification is $50/0.30\approx 167$; the
product $\Phi A$ and hence the total photon power measured by the
chamber is conserved, so the $50\,{\rm mm}$ incident size enters only the
geometrical overlap factor already contained in $t_{d}=w_{f}/v$.
Using $W=34\,{\rm eV}$ the often quoted round number is $K_{s}\approx
3.9\;\mu$Gy per 20 ms dwell, $\approx 0.004\,{\rm mGy}$ per single
energy image. ∎

public_text.1:
For an air filled ion chamber the charge which is collected in the time $dt$
is

\begin{equation*}
dQ=e\,dN_{i}=e\,\frac{dE_{abs}}{W}
\end{equation*}

so that with the usual $W_{air}\simeq 33.7\,{\rm eV}= 33.7\,{\rm V}$
for air

\begin{equation*}
P_{abs}= {\dot E}_{abs}=I\,W\qquad 
{\rm  in~W}
\end{equation*}

is the power absorbed in the air which is actually traversed by the
beam.  For a beam which is small compared with the chamber

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}\,A_{f}\,L_{c}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is in the beam at any instant, $A_{f}$ the
geometrical cross section of the beam at the focus and $L_{c}$ the
length of air which is traversed by the beam.  With

* $A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\,{\rm
cm^{2}}$,
* $L_{c}=15.1\,{\rm cm}$,
* $\rho_{air}=1.293\times10^{-3}\,{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}$,

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1
      =3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is being ionised.  The power absorbed in that
mass is

\begin{equation*}
P_{abs}=I\,W=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.7{\rm V}
       \approx6.7\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{air}= \frac{P_{abs}}{m_{air}}
          =\frac{I\,W}{ \rho_{air}A_{f}L_{c}}
          \simeq\frac{6.7\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{tissue}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$
the same kerma rate is obtained in soft tissue to a very good
approximation, the usual $(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ cancelling in the ratio of
kerma to ionisation current,
\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq \dot D_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
        \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,min^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at the focal spot. In terms of a photon fluence $\Phi$

\begin{equation*}
\Phi =\frac{I\,W}{eA_{f}E(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho L_{c}}
     \simeq1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at 33 keV, i.e. $\dot K\simeq\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ as above.

A material point which is scanned under the 0.30 mm focal spot is
illuminated for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v}
     =\frac{w_{f}}{\dot s}
\end{equation*}

where $v$ is the scan speed.  The experiment is quoted as

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/{\rm exposure\;time}}=0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

so that the dose actually received by a surface element during one
energy pass is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. about $3$–$4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic exposure at the
focal spot.  With $W_{air}=33.97{\rm eV}$ the conventional number is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, essentially independent of the quoted $(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ as
long as $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{tissue}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$.  This
is the peak surface dose under the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focal
spot.

For a conventional dual–energy Laue acquisition the dose is
$2D_{1}\simeq7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ and for a three–energy material
decomposition acquisition $3D_{1}\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm
Gy}\sim10\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per scanned point, i.e. a few $10^{-5}$ Gy
total, which is the order of magnitude normally quoted for
synchrotron multi-energy micro–tomography.  The $50\,{\rm mm}$ incident
beam is the field which is covered by the scan, the $0.30/50\simeq
6\times10^{-3}$ geometric dilution giving a mean dose over the $50\times
6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ field of $D_{1}w_{f}/W_{0}\simeq2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\rm
Gy\approx20\!-\!30\,nGy$ per energy, essentially the conventional
“average over the imaged area” figure.  The $3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
energy quoted above is the local surface dose under the focal spot
which is the quantity normally used in radiation protection for the
scan. ∎

public_text.9:
In an air filled chamber the charge which is collected per unit time is

\begin{equation*}
I=\dot Q =\dot N_{i}\,e
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}= \dot N_{i}W = I\,W/e
\end{equation*}

is the rate at which energy is put into the air which is seen by the
beam.  With the usual $W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm eV}$ per ion pair

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}= I\frac{W}{e}= I\times33.97\;{\rm V}
        =2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97=6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

is the power which is recorded in the chamber.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with the $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ air column which is seen

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L =0.2718\;{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad 
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.2718
      =3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the chamber is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{air}}{m_{air}}
           =\frac{IW/e}{m_{air}}
           \approx\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
           \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $\mu_{en}/\rho_{t}\simeq\mu_{en}/\rho_{air}$ the
absorbed dose rate in tissue at the focus is essentially the same
air kerma rate.

For a horizontal scan the dwell time of a given point on the surface
under the stationary beam is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{\dot s}= \frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/t_{exp}}
\end{equation*}

and the quoted

\begin{equation*}
\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/t_{exp}}=0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

is the dwell time.  Hence the dose delivered to one illuminated
spot in one energy acquisition is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq\dot K_{air}\,t_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\sim 4\;\mu$Gy per beam dwell at the surface, essentially the
air kerma rate quoted above.  This is the conventional $\simeq$ 
$3\!-\!4\;\mu$Gy per mono-energetic exposure which is normally quoted
for a 33 keV Laue beam of the quoted intensity.

If the dose is integrated over the field which is covered by a
horizontal scan the number of distinct beam positions needed to cover
the 50 mm illumination at the monochromator is

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}\approx\frac{50.0\;{\rm mm}}{0.30\;{\rm mm}}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

so that the integrated energy put into the $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$
field in one energy acquisition is $N_{h}D_{1}$.  In integrated terms

\begin{equation*}
D_{int}\approx N_{h}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
       \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}$ absorbed in the $3\,{\rm cm^{2}}$
surface which is scanned in one mono-energetic pass.  For the usual
two–three energy multi-energy acquisition the point dose is $nD_{1}$,
e.g. $\approx7.7\;\mu$Gy for a dual energy acquisition and
$\approx11\!-\!12\;\mu$Gy for a three energy acquisition, the
integrated 50 mm field being $\approx1.3\!-\!2.0\;{\rm mGy}$,
respectively.  The $\sim4\;\mu$Gy per dwell is the often quoted
surface kerma per mono-energetic image.

1.text:
The ionisation which is collected in the air of the chamber is the
energy which is actually put into the air which is instantaneously in
the beam.

$\dot E_{a}=N_{i}W=\dfrac{I}{e}W = I\,W/e$ , $W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}$ for
air

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
       \simeq6.7\!-\!6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}
is the power absorbed in the air volume which is seen by the focused
beam.

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
    =1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}\times15.1{\rm cm}
     =0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272{\rm
cm^{3}}\simeq3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}
is the mass of air which is in the beam at any instant.  Hence the
instantaneous air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
         =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
         \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
         \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$\simeq0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$, $k_{en}L\simeq6.4\times10^{-3}\ll1$, so
that the entrance kerma is essentially the mean kerma in the 15.1 cm
of air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the same
number is the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue at the focus,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the instantaneous $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot.  In fluence
form $\dot D=\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV this is $\Phi_{0}\sim10^{12}\;{\rm m^{-2}
s^{-1}}$ at the focus.

A surface element is in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}=w_{f}/[\Delta x/t_{exp}]\simeq0.020\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dwell quoted in the statement.  The peak surface dose which is
deposited in that element during one monochromatic passage through the
focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
      \times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
      \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$
per 20 ms dwell per energy step, essentially independent of the quoted
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ under the $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ approximation.  With $W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm eV}$
the conventional round number is

$D_{1}\simeq3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy per
surface point, $0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous peak dose rate
at the focus.

The dose is additive with the number of energies used in the Laue
multi-energy acquisition, $D_{N}\approx N_{E}D_{1}$.  For the usual
dual-energy set $D_{2}\simeq7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point and for a
three-energy material decomposition $D_{3}\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2\times
10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\sim10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per scanned point, i.e.
a few $\times10^{-5}\,$Gy per image, the normally quoted synchrotron
micro-beam level.

If the dose is summed over the whole $W_{i}=50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal
field which is covered by scanning the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ spot,
$N_{h}=W_{i}/w_{f}\simeq50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$ independent
positions are required, $N_{h}t_{d}\simeq3.3\;{\rm s}$ total scan time
per energy.  The energy integrated over the $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
line is

\begin{equation*}
D_{int}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
      {\rm Gy}\simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7
      \ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic energy, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the nominal
numbers, i.e. $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms dwell and $\sim0.6\;{\rm
mGy}$ cumulative air/tissue kerma over the 50 mm scanned line per
energy.  The mean dose averaged over the whole $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
field is $D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\sim2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
20\!-\!30\;{\rm nGy}$ per energy, the familiar $ \sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
projection figure being the local peak dose under the focal spot. ∎

4.text:
In air the power which is registered in the 2.0 pA chamber is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}= I\,\frac{W}{e}=I\times33.97\;{\rm V}
      =2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $I/e=1.25\times10^{7}$ ion pairs s$^{-1}$.

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm
cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that the mass of air which is instantaneously in the beam in the
$L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ chamber is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L=
1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\times15.1{\rm cm}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}\; .
\end{equation*}

The air kerma rate in the focal spot is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
\approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
\approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm
cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV the same instantaneous absorbed dose rate is
taken for soft tissue,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the centre of the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  $k_{en}L=
(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho L\approx6.4\times10^{-3}\ll1$ so that the 15.1 cm air
column attenuates the beam by $<1\%$ and the entrance kerma can be used.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}=w_{f}/{\rm scan\;step/exp\;time}\approx2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dwell quoted in the statement.  The dose delivered to one surface
element on one passage through the monochromatic focus, i.e. one energy
exposure, is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
       \times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}\approx3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

essentially

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx
3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per 20 ms dwell per monochromatic energy at the focal spot.  This is the
peak surface air kerma normally quoted for the 33 keV, 2 pA Laue
micro-beam,
$\dot D_{t}\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous, $D_{1}\simeq
4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy per imaged point.

For a conventional $N_{E}$-energy Laue acquisition the point dose is
additive, $D_{N_{E}}\simeq N_{E}D_{1}$; e.g.

* $N_{E}=2$: $D_{2}\simeq7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx7.8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point,
* $N_{E}=3$: $D_{3}\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\approx11\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point,

the familiar few $\mu$Gy per projection level for synchrotron multi-energy
radiography.

If the dose is summed over the whole $W_{i}=50\;{\rm mm}$ monochromator
illuminated width which is covered by scanning the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$
focus, $N_{h}=W_{i}/w_{f}\approx50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$ steps are
required.  The energy integrated over the scanned $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
line per energy channel is

\begin{equation*}
D_{int}=N_{h}D_{1}\approx167\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
       \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

on the surface, $\simeq0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the nominal numbers, i.e.
$\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per step and $\sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ cumulative over the
complete 50 mm Laue bandwidth per energy channel.  The time averaged dose
over the whole imaged area is $D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm
Gy}\approx20\!-\!30\;{\rm nGy}$ per energy, $\approx46\;{\rm nGy}$ for a
dual-energy set, the usual mean-over-field figure.  The $3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
per energy per 0.02 s dwell is the local surface dose under the
$0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focal spot. ∎

5.text:
The ionisation current can be written as an energy absorption rate in the
air that is seen by the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W/e\;, \qquad W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
        \approx 6.7\!-\!6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the volume which is intersected by the focus.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm
cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that with the $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ air column

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}A_{f}L=
1.293\times10^{-3}\times1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1
\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is ionised at any instant.  Hence the
instantaneous air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
        =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
        \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
        \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
        \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

and with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ at 33 keV this
is the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm
mm^{2}}$ spot.  The $k_{en}L=(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho L\simeq6\times10^{-3}\ll1$,
so the average over the 15.1 cm of air is $ \dot K_{a}$ to $<1\%$.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v=\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/t_{exp}}\approx0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dwell quoted in the statement.  The peak surface dose deposited in
one monochromatic passage, i.e. per energy step of the Laue fan, is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
\dot K_{a}\,t_{d}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$ per
$20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell per energy per surface point, the usual
$\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak air-tissue kerma rate at the
synchrotron focus.  With $W=33.97\;{\rm eV}$ this is $D_{1}\simeq
3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV exposure.

In particular for the nominal dual-energy acquisition

\begin{equation*}
D_{2E}\simeq2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per point and $D_{3E}\simeq3D_{1}\approx1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx11\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point for a three-energy material
decomposition set, the familiar few $\mu{\rm Gy}$ synchrotron imaging
level.

Integrated over the whole $W_{i}=50\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal field which is
mapped into the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus,
$N_{h}=W_{i}/w_{f}\approx50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$ independent
positions are traversed.  The kerma summed over the complete dispersed
fan per energy scan is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm int}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}
\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ surface air kerma for the $50\times6\;{\rm
mm^{2}}$ field per monochromatic pass, i.e. $\approx2\!-\!3\;{\rm mGy}$
for a conventional dual/triple energy Laue acquisition.  The mean dose
over the $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ field per energy is
$D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}\approx20\!-\!30\;{\rm
nGy}$ per energy, the $3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell
being the local peak surface dose normally quoted for the multiple
energy system.  This is independent of the quoted $(\mu_{en}/\rho)$
to the accuracy $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$, and
scales linearly with the number $N_{E}$ of distinct energies used in
the acquisition. ∎

12.text:
For an air filled chamber the ionisation rate is the energy which is
actually removed from the beam in the mass of air which is seen by the
beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}= \frac{W}{e}I\;, \qquad W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V\;air}
\end{equation*}

so that with $I=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\approx6.8\times10^{-11}
\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}
is the power deposited in the air which is in the beam.

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}
{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with $L_{c}=15.1\,{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L_{c}
      =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
        \times15.1{\rm cm}
      \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}
is the mass of air which is ionised at any instant.  The instantaneous
air kerma and, with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$, the
absorbed dose rate in soft tissue at the focal plane is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{0}\simeq\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
       =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L_{c}}
       \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
       \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\,\dot D_{0}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ at the $0.30\times6.0\,
{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\,{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$
$k_{en}L_{c}\approx6\times10^{-3}\ll1$ so that attenuation in the
$15.1\,{\rm cm}$ air column is negligible and
$\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}$.

A surface element stays under the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan
for the dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/t_{exp}}
      \approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}
quoted in the statement.  The kerma deposited in that element during
one monochromatic passage is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{0}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

$D_{1}\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\,{\rm mGy}$ per
$20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell per energy, essentially the peak surface air
kerma that the $2.0\,{\rm pA}$ current implies at $33\,{\rm keV}$ in the
Laue focus.  This is the conventional $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy
per imaged point, $ \dot D_{0}\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous
peak rate.

In particular $D_{2E}\approx2D_{1}\sim7\!-\!8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ and
$D_{3E}\approx3D_{1}\sim11\!-\!12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per scanned point for the
usual dual/triple energy Laue acquisition.  The $50\,{\rm mm}$ incident
width is covered by $N_{h}\simeq W_{i}/w_{f}=50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$
independent focal positions.  The energy summed over the whole
$50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ scanned line per monochromatic view is

\begin{equation*}
D_{int}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
{\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}
$\approx0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ per energy, i.e. a mean dose over the whole
field of $D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}{\rm Gy}\approx
20\!-\!30\,{\rm nGy}$ per energy per point, the usual $\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$
per projection level quoted for synchrotron multiple-energy micro
radiography.  The $3\!-\!4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $0.02\,{\rm s}$ dwell is the
local surface dose normally quoted for the scan; it scales linearly with
the number $N_{E}$ of monochromatic energies used, $D_{N_{E}}=N_{E}D_{1}$. ∎

2.text:
In the air which is actually in the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
      \approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30{\rm mm}\times 6.0{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm
cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L
     =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm
cm^{2}}\times15.1{\rm cm}
     \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the instantaneous air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \dot E_{a}/m_{a}= \frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
       \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
       \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ 
       {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\ 
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV the same rate is the absorbed dose rate
in soft tissue at the focus,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

$k_{en}L\approx6.4\times10^{-3}\ll1$ so that the $15.1\;{\rm cm}$ of air
in the chamber is essentially non absorbing.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan
for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/{\rm exp\;time}}\approx
2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

so that the peak dose received by the element on one monochromatic
passage is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
       \times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm
Gy}\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy step, i.e. $ \approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times0.020\,{\rm
s}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\,{\rm mGy}$ per  20 ms dwell.  This is the
conventional $ \sim 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic projection surface
kerma that the $2.0\;{\rm pA}$ current implies at 33 keV.  With
$N_{E}$ independent energy steps the point dose scales as $N_{E}D_{1}$,
e.g. $2D_{1}\approx7.6\!-\!8.0\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a dual–energy Laue
acquisition and $3D_{1}\approx11\!-\!12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a three energy
material decomposition set.

On the $50\,{\rm mm}$ monochromator illumination the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$
focus samples

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}\simeq\frac{W_{i}}{w_{f}}\approx\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

independent positions.  The energy summed over the whole dispersed
field in one monochromatic scan is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm int}\approx N_{h}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times
10^{-6}{\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7
\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per energy pass to the $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ scanned line,
$\approx0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ with the nominal numbers.  The mean dose
averaged over the $50\,{\rm mm}$ field is
$D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}{\rm Gy}\approx20\!-\!30\ 
{\rm nGy}$ per energy, the $ \sim 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$
dwell being the local surface dose normally quoted for the synchrotron
Laue micro-beam. ∎

3.text:
The power which is actually put into the air which is in the beam at the
focus is obtained from the ionisation current

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}= \frac{W}{e}I ,\qquad W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
        \approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W},
\end{equation*}
is the energy absorbed per second in the air volume

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}},\qquad 
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272
     \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}\; .
\end{equation*}

Hence the air kerma rate in the instantaneous focal spot and, with
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ , the absorbed dose rate
in soft tissue in the spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{0}= \dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
         =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
         \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
         \approx1.9-2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\dot D_{0}\simeq0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak air/tissue kerma rate
at the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  In fluence form

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{0}= \Phi E_{0}\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{a},
\qquad
\Phi\sim10^{12}{\rm m^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at $E_{0}=33.0\,{\rm keV}$ with
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\,{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$, $k_{en}L\simeq6\times10^{-3}\ll1$
so that the entrance kerma is essentially the mean kerma in the 15.1 cm
air column.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}= \frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{exp}}\approx0.020\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}
per exposure.  The surface kerma, i.e. the absorbed dose to the surface
layer of tissue, deposited in one monochromatic passage of the spot is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{0}t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
       \times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq 3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}
per $0.02\,{\rm s}$ dwell per energy at the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$
focus.  This is the local peak surface dose normally quoted for the
synchrotron Laue micro-beam.  With the conventional $W_{air}=33.97\,{\rm
eV}$ this is $\approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times0.020\,{\rm s}$.

For the usual multi-energy Laue acquisition the dose is additive.
$N_{E}=2$ gives $D_{2}\!\simeq\!7\!-\!8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point and
$N_{E}=3$ gives $D_{3}\!\simeq\!1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}{\rm Gy}\approx
11\!-\!12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per scanned point, i.e. a few $\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
projection, the $ \sim 10^{-5}{\rm Gy}$ level normally quoted for
synchrotron multiple-energy radiography.

If the kerma is integrated over the whole horizontal fan which is covered
by scanning the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ spot over the $W_{i}=50\,{\rm mm}$
monochromator illumination,

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}= \frac{W_{i}}{w_{f}}\approx\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

the energy deposited in the scanned $50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ line per
energy channel is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm int}\approx N_{h}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
       \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ surface air/tissue kerma accumulated in the
$50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ scanned line per monochromatic energy,
equivalent to a mean over the field of $D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\approx2\!-\!3
\times10^{-8}{\rm Gy}\approx20\!-\!30\,{\rm nGy}$ per energy per point.
The $ \approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell is the customary
peak local surface dose quoted for the $33\,{\rm keV}$, $2.0\,{\rm pA}$
Laue system; the $ \sim0.6\,{\rm mGy}$ figure is the corresponding
integrated surface kerma over the full $50\,{\rm mm}$ Laue fan. ∎

15.text:
In air the power which is recorded in the ionisation chamber is the
energy which is actually put into the air which is seen by the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W/e\;\;\qquad W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V\;air}
\end{equation*}

so that with $I=2.0\,{\rm pA}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

is the power absorbed in the air volume which is in the beam.  At the
focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm
cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that the mass of air which is irradiated at any instant in the
$L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ chamber is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L
     =1.293\times10^{-3}\ {\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm
cm^{2}}\times15.1\ {\rm cm}
     \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}\; .
\end{equation*}

The instantaneous air-kerma, and with
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the instantaneous
absorbed dose rate in soft tissue, in the focal spot is therefore

\begin{equation*}
 \dot D_{0}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
 \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
 \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
 \simeq0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} 
\end{equation*}

in the beam centre.  In the usual $\dot D=\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ form
the $ (\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ cancels in the
ratio $ \dot D/I$ and $k_{en}L\simeq6\times10^{-3}\ll1$ so that the
air column attenuation in the 15.1 cm of air is negligible.

A surface element which is stepped through the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$
horizontal fan stays in the beam for the dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{exp}}\simeq0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

which is the number quoted in the statement.  The absorbed dose
deposited in that element on one passage of the monochromatic spot,
i.e. the surface dose for one energy step of the Laue system, is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{0}\;t_{d}
     \simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
     \simeq3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. about

\[
D_{1}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}
\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times0.02\,{\rm s}
\]

per 20 ms dwell per monochromatic energy at the focal spot.  With
$W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm eV}$ the conventionally quoted number is
$D_{1}\simeq3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}\simeq3.9\times10^{-6}\,{\rm Gy}$ per
energy per surface element, essentially independent of the quoted
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ because $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$.
For a conventional two energy Laue acquisition this is
$\approx2D_{1}\simeq7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point and
$\approx3D_{1}\simeq11\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a three-energy
material decomposition set, the familiar few $\mu$Gy per projection
synchrotron micro-beam level.

If the dose is summed over the whole $W_{i}=50\;{\rm mm}$ Laue fan
which is mapped into the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focus,
$N_{h}=W_{i}/w_{f}\simeq50/0.30\simeq1.67\times10^{2}$ independent
beam widths are stepped over the surface.  The kerma integrated over
the whole $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ scanned line is

\[
D_{\rm int}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}\simeq167\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\]

per monochromatic pass, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers
quoted above, i.e. $\sim 23\,{\rm nGy}$ mean dose per energy averaged
over the $50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ field,
$D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}$.  This is the $\simeq0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ integrated
surface air kerma normally quoted for the complete horizontal Laue
scan; the $\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms dwell is the local peak
surface dose per energy which is normally used for radiation
protection.  With $n_{E}$ energies the point dose scales as
$n_{E}D_{1}$ and the integrated field dose as $n_{E}D_{\rm int}$.

6.text:
In the air which is seen by the focus the power which is put in by the
$2.0\;{\rm pA}$ beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,\frac{W}{e}=I\times33.97\;{\rm V}\approx
2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W} ,
\end{equation*}
$W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air.  The mass of air which is in the beam
at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\;
{\rm cm^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx3.5\times
10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg},
\end{equation*}
so that the instantaneous air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}\approx
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\approx
1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}
With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the same number is
the absorbed-dose rate in unit density soft tissue at the focus,
$\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}$.  The $k_{en}L\simeq6.4\times10^{-3}$ air
attenuation in the $15.1\,{\rm cm}$ column is $<1\%$.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\equiv\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/t_{exp}}\approx
2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s} ,
\end{equation*}
the dwell quoted for the scan.  Hence the absorbed dose delivered to a
given surface element on one monochromatic passage through the focus is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}=\dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\,\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$ per
$20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell per energy, $\,\dot D_{t}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
peak.  This is the familiar $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection surface
kerma for the $33\ {\rm keV}$ Laue focus.  On a $N_{E}$ energy
acquisition the point dose scales as $N_{E}D_{1}$, e.g. $2D_{1}\simeq
7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for dual energy and $3D_{1}\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2\times
10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\sim10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a three energy material
decomposition set, the $4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy being the quantity
normally quoted for radiation protection of the micro-beam.

In integrated form the $50\,{\rm mm}$ monochromator width is compressed
to the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus, $N_{h}=W_{i}/w_{f}\approx50/0.30\approx
1.67\times10^{2}$ independent horizontal positions.  The kerma summed
over the whole $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ Laue fan in one mono-energetic
scan is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm int}\approx N_{h}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times
3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}\approx0.64\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}
per energy, i.e. $D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx20\!-\!30\;{\rm nGy}$ mean dose over the whole $50\times6\,
{\rm mm^{2}}$ field per energy.  This $0.6\,{\rm mGy}$ integrated over the
Laue dispersed field is the conventionally quoted cumulative surface
kerma for the full multiple-energy scan; the $ \approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
per $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell is the peak local surface dose per energy
projection. ∎

public_text.1:
The ionisation which is collected in the air of the chamber is the
energy which is actually put into the air which is instantaneously in
the beam.

$\dot E_{a}=N_{i}W=\dfrac{I}{e}W = I\,W/e$ , $W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}$ for
air

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
       \simeq6.7\!-\!6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}
is the power absorbed in the air volume which is seen by the focused
beam.

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
    =1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}\times15.1{\rm cm}
     =0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272{\rm
cm^{3}}\simeq3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}
is the mass of air which is in the beam at any instant.  Hence the
instantaneous air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
         =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
         \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
         \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$\simeq0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$, $k_{en}L\simeq6.4\times10^{-3}\ll1$, so
that the entrance kerma is essentially the mean kerma in the 15.1 cm
of air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the same
number is the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue at the focus,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the instantaneous $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot.  In fluence
form $\dot D=\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV this is $\Phi_{0}\sim10^{12}\;{\rm m^{-2}
s^{-1}}$ at the focus.

A surface element is in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}=w_{f}/[\Delta x/t_{exp}]\simeq0.020\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dwell quoted in the statement.  The peak surface dose which is
deposited in that element during one monochromatic passage through the
focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
      \times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
      \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$
per 20 ms dwell per energy step, essentially independent of the quoted
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ under the $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ approximation.  With $W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm eV}$
the conventional round number is

$D_{1}\simeq3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy per
surface point, $0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous peak dose rate
at the focus.

The dose is additive with the number of energies used in the Laue
multi-energy acquisition, $D_{N}\approx N_{E}D_{1}$.  For the usual
dual-energy set $D_{2}\simeq7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point and for a
three-energy material decomposition $D_{3}\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2\times
10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\sim10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per scanned point, i.e.
a few $\times10^{-5}\,$Gy per image, the normally quoted synchrotron
micro-beam level.

If the dose is summed over the whole $W_{i}=50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal
field which is covered by scanning the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ spot,
$N_{h}=W_{i}/w_{f}\simeq50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$ independent
positions are required, $N_{h}t_{d}\simeq3.3\;{\rm s}$ total scan time
per energy.  The energy integrated over the $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
line is

\begin{equation*}
D_{int}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
      {\rm Gy}\simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7
      \ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic energy, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the nominal
numbers, i.e. $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms dwell and $\sim0.6\;{\rm
mGy}$ cumulative air/tissue kerma over the 50 mm scanned line per
energy.  The mean dose averaged over the whole $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
field is $D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\sim2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
20\!-\!30\;{\rm nGy}$ per energy, the familiar $ \sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
projection figure being the local peak dose under the focal spot. ∎

public_text.4:
In air the power which is registered in the 2.0 pA chamber is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}= I\,\frac{W}{e}=I\times33.97\;{\rm V}
      =2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $I/e=1.25\times10^{7}$ ion pairs s$^{-1}$.

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm
cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that the mass of air which is instantaneously in the beam in the
$L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ chamber is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L=
1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\times15.1{\rm cm}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}\; .
\end{equation*}

The air kerma rate in the focal spot is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
\approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
\approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm
cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV the same instantaneous absorbed dose rate is
taken for soft tissue,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the centre of the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  $k_{en}L=
(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho L\approx6.4\times10^{-3}\ll1$ so that the 15.1 cm air
column attenuates the beam by $<1\%$ and the entrance kerma can be used.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}=w_{f}/{\rm scan\;step/exp\;time}\approx2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dwell quoted in the statement.  The dose delivered to one surface
element on one passage through the monochromatic focus, i.e. one energy
exposure, is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
       \times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}\approx3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

essentially

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx
3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per 20 ms dwell per monochromatic energy at the focal spot.  This is the
peak surface air kerma normally quoted for the 33 keV, 2 pA Laue
micro-beam,
$\dot D_{t}\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous, $D_{1}\simeq
4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy per imaged point.

For a conventional $N_{E}$-energy Laue acquisition the point dose is
additive, $D_{N_{E}}\simeq N_{E}D_{1}$; e.g.

* $N_{E}=2$: $D_{2}\simeq7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx7.8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point,
* $N_{E}=3$: $D_{3}\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\approx11\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point,

the familiar few $\mu$Gy per projection level for synchrotron multi-energy
radiography.

If the dose is summed over the whole $W_{i}=50\;{\rm mm}$ monochromator
illuminated width which is covered by scanning the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$
focus, $N_{h}=W_{i}/w_{f}\approx50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$ steps are
required.  The energy integrated over the scanned $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
line per energy channel is

\begin{equation*}
D_{int}=N_{h}D_{1}\approx167\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
       \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

on the surface, $\simeq0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the nominal numbers, i.e.
$\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per step and $\sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ cumulative over the
complete 50 mm Laue bandwidth per energy channel.  The time averaged dose
over the whole imaged area is $D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm
Gy}\approx20\!-\!30\;{\rm nGy}$ per energy, $\approx46\;{\rm nGy}$ for a
dual-energy set, the usual mean-over-field figure.  The $3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
per energy per 0.02 s dwell is the local surface dose under the
$0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focal spot. ∎

public_text.5:
The ionisation current can be written as an energy absorption rate in the
air that is seen by the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W/e\;, \qquad W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
        \approx 6.7\!-\!6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the volume which is intersected by the focus.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm
cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that with the $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ air column

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}A_{f}L=
1.293\times10^{-3}\times1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1
\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is ionised at any instant.  Hence the
instantaneous air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
        =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
        \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
        \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
        \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

and with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ at 33 keV this
is the absorbed dose rate in soft tissue in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm
mm^{2}}$ spot.  The $k_{en}L=(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho L\simeq6\times10^{-3}\ll1$,
so the average over the 15.1 cm of air is $ \dot K_{a}$ to $<1\%$.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v=\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/t_{exp}}\approx0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dwell quoted in the statement.  The peak surface dose deposited in
one monochromatic passage, i.e. per energy step of the Laue fan, is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
\dot K_{a}\,t_{d}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$ per
$20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell per energy per surface point, the usual
$\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak air-tissue kerma rate at the
synchrotron focus.  With $W=33.97\;{\rm eV}$ this is $D_{1}\simeq
3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV exposure.

In particular for the nominal dual-energy acquisition

\begin{equation*}
D_{2E}\simeq2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per point and $D_{3E}\simeq3D_{1}\approx1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx11\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point for a three-energy material
decomposition set, the familiar few $\mu{\rm Gy}$ synchrotron imaging
level.

Integrated over the whole $W_{i}=50\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal field which is
mapped into the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus,
$N_{h}=W_{i}/w_{f}\approx50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$ independent
positions are traversed.  The kerma summed over the complete dispersed
fan per energy scan is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm int}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}
\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ surface air kerma for the $50\times6\;{\rm
mm^{2}}$ field per monochromatic pass, i.e. $\approx2\!-\!3\;{\rm mGy}$
for a conventional dual/triple energy Laue acquisition.  The mean dose
over the $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ field per energy is
$D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}\approx20\!-\!30\;{\rm
nGy}$ per energy, the $3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell
being the local peak surface dose normally quoted for the multiple
energy system.  This is independent of the quoted $(\mu_{en}/\rho)$
to the accuracy $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$, and
scales linearly with the number $N_{E}$ of distinct energies used in
the acquisition. ∎

public_text.12:
For an air filled chamber the ionisation rate is the energy which is
actually removed from the beam in the mass of air which is seen by the
beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}= \frac{W}{e}I\;, \qquad W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V\;air}
\end{equation*}

so that with $I=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\approx6.8\times10^{-11}
\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}
is the power deposited in the air which is in the beam.

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}
{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with $L_{c}=15.1\,{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L_{c}
      =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
        \times15.1{\rm cm}
      \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}
is the mass of air which is ionised at any instant.  The instantaneous
air kerma and, with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$, the
absorbed dose rate in soft tissue at the focal plane is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{0}\simeq\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
       =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L_{c}}
       \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
       \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\,\dot D_{0}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ at the $0.30\times6.0\,
{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\,{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$
$k_{en}L_{c}\approx6\times10^{-3}\ll1$ so that attenuation in the
$15.1\,{\rm cm}$ air column is negligible and
$\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}$.

A surface element stays under the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan
for the dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/t_{exp}}
      \approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}
quoted in the statement.  The kerma deposited in that element during
one monochromatic passage is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{0}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

$D_{1}\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\,{\rm mGy}$ per
$20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell per energy, essentially the peak surface air
kerma that the $2.0\,{\rm pA}$ current implies at $33\,{\rm keV}$ in the
Laue focus.  This is the conventional $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy
per imaged point, $ \dot D_{0}\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous
peak rate.

In particular $D_{2E}\approx2D_{1}\sim7\!-\!8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ and
$D_{3E}\approx3D_{1}\sim11\!-\!12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per scanned point for the
usual dual/triple energy Laue acquisition.  The $50\,{\rm mm}$ incident
width is covered by $N_{h}\simeq W_{i}/w_{f}=50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$
independent focal positions.  The energy summed over the whole
$50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ scanned line per monochromatic view is

\begin{equation*}
D_{int}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
{\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}
$\approx0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ per energy, i.e. a mean dose over the whole
field of $D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}{\rm Gy}\approx
20\!-\!30\,{\rm nGy}$ per energy per point, the usual $\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$
per projection level quoted for synchrotron multiple-energy micro
radiography.  The $3\!-\!4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $0.02\,{\rm s}$ dwell is the
local surface dose normally quoted for the scan; it scales linearly with
the number $N_{E}$ of monochromatic energies used, $D_{N_{E}}=N_{E}D_{1}$. ∎

public_text.2:
In the air which is actually in the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
      \approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30{\rm mm}\times 6.0{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm
cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L
     =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm
cm^{2}}\times15.1{\rm cm}
     \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the instantaneous air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \dot E_{a}/m_{a}= \frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
       \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
       \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ 
       {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\ 
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV the same rate is the absorbed dose rate
in soft tissue at the focus,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

$k_{en}L\approx6.4\times10^{-3}\ll1$ so that the $15.1\;{\rm cm}$ of air
in the chamber is essentially non absorbing.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan
for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/{\rm exp\;time}}\approx
2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

so that the peak dose received by the element on one monochromatic
passage is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
       \times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm
Gy}\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy step, i.e. $ \approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times0.020\,{\rm
s}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\,{\rm mGy}$ per  20 ms dwell.  This is the
conventional $ \sim 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic projection surface
kerma that the $2.0\;{\rm pA}$ current implies at 33 keV.  With
$N_{E}$ independent energy steps the point dose scales as $N_{E}D_{1}$,
e.g. $2D_{1}\approx7.6\!-\!8.0\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a dual–energy Laue
acquisition and $3D_{1}\approx11\!-\!12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a three energy
material decomposition set.

On the $50\,{\rm mm}$ monochromator illumination the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$
focus samples

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}\simeq\frac{W_{i}}{w_{f}}\approx\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

independent positions.  The energy summed over the whole dispersed
field in one monochromatic scan is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm int}\approx N_{h}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times
10^{-6}{\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7
\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per energy pass to the $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ scanned line,
$\approx0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ with the nominal numbers.  The mean dose
averaged over the $50\,{\rm mm}$ field is
$D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}{\rm Gy}\approx20\!-\!30\ 
{\rm nGy}$ per energy, the $ \sim 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$
dwell being the local surface dose normally quoted for the synchrotron
Laue micro-beam. ∎

public_text.3:
The power which is actually put into the air which is in the beam at the
focus is obtained from the ionisation current

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}= \frac{W}{e}I ,\qquad W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
        \approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W},
\end{equation*}
is the energy absorbed per second in the air volume

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}},\qquad 
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272
     \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}\; .
\end{equation*}

Hence the air kerma rate in the instantaneous focal spot and, with
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ , the absorbed dose rate
in soft tissue in the spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{0}= \dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
         =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
         \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
         \approx1.9-2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\dot D_{0}\simeq0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak air/tissue kerma rate
at the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  In fluence form

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{0}= \Phi E_{0}\Bigl(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Bigr)_{a},
\qquad
\Phi\sim10^{12}{\rm m^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at $E_{0}=33.0\,{\rm keV}$ with
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\,{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$, $k_{en}L\simeq6\times10^{-3}\ll1$
so that the entrance kerma is essentially the mean kerma in the 15.1 cm
air column.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}= \frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{exp}}\approx0.020\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}
per exposure.  The surface kerma, i.e. the absorbed dose to the surface
layer of tissue, deposited in one monochromatic passage of the spot is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{0}t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
       \times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq 3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}{\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}
per $0.02\,{\rm s}$ dwell per energy at the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$
focus.  This is the local peak surface dose normally quoted for the
synchrotron Laue micro-beam.  With the conventional $W_{air}=33.97\,{\rm
eV}$ this is $\approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times0.020\,{\rm s}$.

For the usual multi-energy Laue acquisition the dose is additive.
$N_{E}=2$ gives $D_{2}\!\simeq\!7\!-\!8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point and
$N_{E}=3$ gives $D_{3}\!\simeq\!1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}{\rm Gy}\approx
11\!-\!12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per scanned point, i.e. a few $\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
projection, the $ \sim 10^{-5}{\rm Gy}$ level normally quoted for
synchrotron multiple-energy radiography.

If the kerma is integrated over the whole horizontal fan which is covered
by scanning the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ spot over the $W_{i}=50\,{\rm mm}$
monochromator illumination,

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}= \frac{W_{i}}{w_{f}}\approx\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

the energy deposited in the scanned $50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ line per
energy channel is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm int}\approx N_{h}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
       \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ surface air/tissue kerma accumulated in the
$50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ scanned line per monochromatic energy,
equivalent to a mean over the field of $D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\approx2\!-\!3
\times10^{-8}{\rm Gy}\approx20\!-\!30\,{\rm nGy}$ per energy per point.
The $ \approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell is the customary
peak local surface dose quoted for the $33\,{\rm keV}$, $2.0\,{\rm pA}$
Laue system; the $ \sim0.6\,{\rm mGy}$ figure is the corresponding
integrated surface kerma over the full $50\,{\rm mm}$ Laue fan. ∎

public_text.15:
In air the power which is recorded in the ionisation chamber is the
energy which is actually put into the air which is seen by the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W/e\;\;\qquad W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V\;air}
\end{equation*}

so that with $I=2.0\,{\rm pA}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

is the power absorbed in the air volume which is in the beam.  At the
focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm
cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that the mass of air which is irradiated at any instant in the
$L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ chamber is

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L
     =1.293\times10^{-3}\ {\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm
cm^{2}}\times15.1\ {\rm cm}
     \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}\; .
\end{equation*}

The instantaneous air-kerma, and with
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the instantaneous
absorbed dose rate in soft tissue, in the focal spot is therefore

\begin{equation*}
 \dot D_{0}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
 \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
 \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
 \simeq0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} 
\end{equation*}

in the beam centre.  In the usual $\dot D=\Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ form
the $ (\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ cancels in the
ratio $ \dot D/I$ and $k_{en}L\simeq6\times10^{-3}\ll1$ so that the
air column attenuation in the 15.1 cm of air is negligible.

A surface element which is stepped through the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$
horizontal fan stays in the beam for the dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{exp}}\simeq0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

which is the number quoted in the statement.  The absorbed dose
deposited in that element on one passage of the monochromatic spot,
i.e. the surface dose for one energy step of the Laue system, is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{0}\;t_{d}
     \simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
     \simeq3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. about

\[
D_{1}\approx3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}
\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times0.02\,{\rm s}
\]

per 20 ms dwell per monochromatic energy at the focal spot.  With
$W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm eV}$ the conventionally quoted number is
$D_{1}\simeq3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}\simeq3.9\times10^{-6}\,{\rm Gy}$ per
energy per surface element, essentially independent of the quoted
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ because $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$.
For a conventional two energy Laue acquisition this is
$\approx2D_{1}\simeq7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point and
$\approx3D_{1}\simeq11\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a three-energy
material decomposition set, the familiar few $\mu$Gy per projection
synchrotron micro-beam level.

If the dose is summed over the whole $W_{i}=50\;{\rm mm}$ Laue fan
which is mapped into the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focus,
$N_{h}=W_{i}/w_{f}\simeq50/0.30\simeq1.67\times10^{2}$ independent
beam widths are stepped over the surface.  The kerma integrated over
the whole $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ scanned line is

\[
D_{\rm int}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}\simeq167\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\]

per monochromatic pass, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers
quoted above, i.e. $\sim 23\,{\rm nGy}$ mean dose per energy averaged
over the $50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ field,
$D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}$.  This is the $\simeq0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ integrated
surface air kerma normally quoted for the complete horizontal Laue
scan; the $\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms dwell is the local peak
surface dose per energy which is normally used for radiation
protection.  With $n_{E}$ energies the point dose scales as
$n_{E}D_{1}$ and the integrated field dose as $n_{E}D_{\rm int}$.

public_text.6:
In the air which is seen by the focus the power which is put in by the
$2.0\;{\rm pA}$ beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,\frac{W}{e}=I\times33.97\;{\rm V}\approx
2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W} ,
\end{equation*}
$W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air.  The mass of air which is in the beam
at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\;
{\rm cm^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx3.5\times
10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg},
\end{equation*}
so that the instantaneous air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}\approx
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\approx
1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}
With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the same number is
the absorbed-dose rate in unit density soft tissue at the focus,
$\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}$.  The $k_{en}L\simeq6.4\times10^{-3}$ air
attenuation in the $15.1\,{\rm cm}$ column is $<1\%$.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\equiv\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/t_{exp}}\approx
2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s} ,
\end{equation*}
the dwell quoted for the scan.  Hence the absorbed dose delivered to a
given surface element on one monochromatic passage through the focus is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}=\dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\,\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$ per
$20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell per energy, $\,\dot D_{t}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
peak.  This is the familiar $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection surface
kerma for the $33\ {\rm keV}$ Laue focus.  On a $N_{E}$ energy
acquisition the point dose scales as $N_{E}D_{1}$, e.g. $2D_{1}\simeq
7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for dual energy and $3D_{1}\simeq1.1\!-\!1.2\times
10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\sim10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a three energy material
decomposition set, the $4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy being the quantity
normally quoted for radiation protection of the micro-beam.

In integrated form the $50\,{\rm mm}$ monochromator width is compressed
to the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus, $N_{h}=W_{i}/w_{f}\approx50/0.30\approx
1.67\times10^{2}$ independent horizontal positions.  The kerma summed
over the whole $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ Laue fan in one mono-energetic
scan is

\begin{equation*}
D_{\rm int}\approx N_{h}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times
3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}\approx0.64\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}
per energy, i.e. $D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx20\!-\!30\;{\rm nGy}$ mean dose over the whole $50\times6\,
{\rm mm^{2}}$ field per energy.  This $0.6\,{\rm mGy}$ integrated over the
Laue dispersed field is the conventionally quoted cumulative surface
kerma for the full multiple-energy scan; the $ \approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
per $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell is the peak local surface dose per energy
projection. ∎

4.text:
The ionisation current is the power which is removed from the beam in the
mass of air which is instantaneously in the $w_{f}\times h_{f}$ focus,

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W/e\; ,
\qquad W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V\;air}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq6.7\!-\!6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the mass

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}}\;,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
        =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

which is seen by the beam at any instant.  Hence the instantaneous air
kerma in the focal spot and, with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$,
the instantaneous absorbed dose rate in unit density soft tissue in the
$0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}\simeq\dot D_{t}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
     =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
     \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
     \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\simeq0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} 
\end{equation*}

peak in the focus.  

$k_{en}L=(\mu_{en}/\rho)\rho L=0.328\times1.293\times10^{-3}\times15.1
\simeq6.4\times10^{-3}\ll1$ so that entrance kerma $\simeq$ mean kerma in the
$15.1\;{\rm cm}$ air column and with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$
the same rate is the soft-tissue absorbed dose rate at the focal plane.

A surface element stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal fan for the
dwell quoted in the statement

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\simeq2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s} ,
\end{equation*}

so that the peak surface kerma deposited in that element on one monochromatic
Laue passage, i.e. one energy step, is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
      \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\simeq3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}
      \simeq0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times0.020\,{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

per $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell per monochromatic energy per scanned point,
the familiar $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection synchrotron micro-beam level
at $33\,{\rm keV},\;2.0\;{\rm pA}$.  The dose is additive with the number
$N_{E}$ of Laue energies used for material decomposition,

\begin{equation*}
D_{N_{E}}=N_{E}D_{1}\;,
\end{equation*}

e.g. for the conventional minimum dual-energy Laue acquisition

\begin{equation*}
D_{2E}\simeq2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx7.6\!-\!8.0\;
\mu{\rm Gy}\ {\rm per\ point},
\end{equation*}

$\dot D_{t}\times0.04\,{\rm s}$ total dwell, and $D_{3E}\simeq3D_{1}\approx
1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\,{\rm Gy}\approx11\!-\!12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point for
a three-energy set.  This is the local peak surface air/tissue kerma
normally quoted for the system.

In field integrated form the $W_{i}=50\,{\rm mm}$ monochromator illumination
is mapped into the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus,

\begin{equation*}
N_{h}=W_{i}/w_{f}\simeq50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\qquad N_{h}t_{d}\simeq3.3\ {\rm s}\ {\rm per\ energy},
\end{equation*}

independent horizontal positions.  Summed over the whole $50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$
scanned Laue fan per monochromatic channel

\begin{equation*}
D_{int}\simeq N_{h}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ surface air/tissue kerma to the complete $50\,{\rm mm}$
Laue fan per energy, i.e. a field mean
$\langle D\rangle_{1}=D_{1}w_{f}/W_{i}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\,{\rm Gy}
\approx20\!-\!30\,{\rm nGy}$ per energy.  With $N_{E}$ energies the
field-integrated cumulative kerma is $N_{E}D_{int}$, $\approx1.2\!-\!1.3\,{\rm mGy}$
over the $50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ line for the usual $N_{E}=2$ dual-energy
acquisition.  The $3\!-\!4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell is the
conventional local peak per energy per point; $N_{E}D_{1}\sim7\!-\!8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
per point for dual energy is the customary cumulative multiple-energy
surface dose quoted for the system.  This is $0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times
0.020\,{\rm s}$ per energy per point, essentially independent of
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)$ under the $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$
approximation. ∎

